INSERT INTO public.quizzes (id, subject, unit, title, description) VALUES ('186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'Chemistry', 'Unit 1', 'Molarum Grade 10 — Chemistry Unit 1: Chemical Reactions and Stoichiometry', 'Source-grounded Molarum Grade 10 Chemistry questions for Unit 1: Chemical Reactions and Stoichiometry.') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET subject = EXCLUDED.subject, unit = EXCLUDED.unit, title = EXCLUDED.title, description = EXCLUDED.description;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, quiz_id, question_type, question_text, correct_answer, explanation, order_index) VALUES ('c3cc7cd5-d964-4d04-8984-e9a3e6490e61'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which balanced equation represents the reaction of hydrogen gas with oxygen to form water?', '2H2 + O2 → 2H2O', 'The balanced equation requires 2 H2 molecules for every O2 to yield 2 H2O; the correct coefficients are 2 and 1 respectively.', 0),('46a76f3a-9ce6-41ec-8bfd-278a9fd422e8'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In a word equation, which part represents the substances that take part in the reaction?', 'Reactant', 'Reactants are written on the left side of the equation to show what starts the reaction.', 1),('88389615-5a40-4c89-8fcc-d7645d30de28'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which of the following is evidence of a chemical change?', 'Rusting of iron', 'Chemical changes involve formation of new substances and other evidences such as color change or heat/light; rusting is a chemical change.', 2),('9abc7544-62e5-433d-8fcb-db99f3ceefa8'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'true_false', 'In a balanced chemical equation, the total number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides.', 'True', 'Mass is conserved in chemical reactions; atoms are rearranged but totals per element remain equal on both sides.', 3),('ef097d7b-fa5f-43ef-8f26-12db234ee407'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'Define the mole and explain why it is central to stoichiometry.', 'The mole is a unit that counts amount of substance as 6.022×10^23 particles; it links mass and number of particles and provides the basis for mole ratios in balanced equations.', 'The mole allows conversion between grams and moles and enables use of coefficients to relate reactants and products in reactions.', 4),('a8dad4dd-27cc-4251-8019-8eca85cb6b33'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'What is the difference between empirical formula and molecular formula?', 'Empirical formula shows the simplest whole-number ratio of elements in a compound; molecular formula shows the actual number of each element in a molecule; they may be the same for some compounds or different for others, and the molecular formula can be derived from the empirical formula using molar mass.', 'Empirical vs molecular formulas convey different levels of information; empirical is a reduced ratio, molecular is the actual composition.', 5),('04efa776-7868-48b2-83e3-b3e22a22bc4d'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Balance the following equation using inspection: Aluminum reacts with oxygen to form aluminum oxide.', '4Al + 3O2 → 2Al2O3', 'Balancing yields 4Al + 3O2 → 2Al2O3; check atoms on both sides.', 6),('c7c71576-ba0b-4258-8bba-e2ae4e245ac8'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In a redox reaction, which species is the oxidizing agent?', 'Cu2+', 'The oxidizing agent is the species that gains electrons; Cu2+ gains electrons to become Cu.', 7),('14ea8eec-99af-4091-8cdc-97cee6e16380'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'A 5.00 g sample of hydrogen gas (H2) reacts with excess oxygen to form water. How many grams of water are produced? Use the reaction: 2 H2 + O2 → 2 H2O. M(H2)=2.016 g/mol; M(H2O)=18.015 g/mol.', '44.68 g of H2O', 'Moles H2 = 5.00 g / 2.016 g/mol = 2.481 mol. From the equation, 2 mol H2 produce 2 mol H2O, so moles H2O = 2.481 mol. Mass = 2.481 × 18.015 = 44.68 g.', 8),('1c6ab12c-ef43-435e-8ea1-3a407f08a4e3'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'A 5.00 g sample of Fe reacts with 10.0 g of O2 to form Fe2O3. Determine the limiting reactant and the theoretical yield of Fe2O3 (MW: Fe=55.85, O=16.00). Balanced equation: 4 Fe + 3 O2 → 2 Fe2O3.', 'Limiting reactant: Fe; Theoretical yield of Fe2O3 ≈ 7.15 g', 'Moles: Fe = 5.00/55.85 = 0.0896 mol; O2 = 10.0/32.00 = 0.3125 mol. For Fe, required O2 = (3/4)*0.0896 = 0.0672 mol; O2 is in excess; Fe is limiting. Moles Fe2O3 = (0.0896)*(2/4) = 0.0448 mol. Mass = 0.0448 × 159.69 ≈ 7.15 g.', 9),('f8bb4435-8ed4-4784-83e4-8f5858ad7316'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which statement correctly describes where reactants and products appear in a chemical equation?', 'Reactants are on the left and products are on the right', 'In a chemical equation, reactants are written on the left side of the arrow and products on the right.', 10),('1c175160-f104-4998-89de-34a3adf3f35f'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In a chemical equation, what does the arrow (→) represent?', 'The reactants transform into products', 'The arrow indicates transformation of reactants into products.', 11),('a6a48117-e8b8-4365-8733-7a6a926e9edb'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which statement best describes balancing chemical equations?', 'It keeps the number of atoms of each element the same on both sides', 'Balancing ensures conservation of atoms; coefficients adjust amounts but not identities.', 12),('ccb9fb1d-fbd2-4768-8ade-e80f893f84ac'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In the reaction Fe2O3 + CO → Fe + CO2, which species acts as the reducing agent?', 'CO', 'CO is oxidized to CO2 and reduces Fe2O3 to Fe, so CO is the reducing agent.', 13),('dd3ddd90-897b-4c02-81e6-79cb5ffbc341'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In a redox reaction using the oxidation-number-change method, which sign of oxidation state change indicates oxidation of an element?', 'Increase in oxidation state', 'Oxidation is an increase in oxidation state; reduction is a decrease.', 14),('a022b4ab-acc8-400a-8c7a-f7c82a173a79'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'true_false', 'In a balanced chemical equation, the total mass of reactants equals the total mass of products.', 'True', 'Conservation of mass requires that mass be conserved in a chemical equation.', 15),('5146a6aa-5b9a-4aaa-8165-0db282b88978'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'Define a chemical equation and identify the reactants and products in a general reaction.', 'A chemical equation uses symbols and formulas to represent a chemical reaction; reactants are written on the left of the arrow and products on the right.', 'A chemical equation expresses the reactants transforming into products.', 16),('4c7209e1-eca9-45e9-86c0-9816220dbc3c'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'Outline the steps to determine the limiting reactant when given masses of two reactants.', '1) Convert the masses to moles for each reactant. 2) Use stoichiometric ratios to determine the amount of product each could form. 3) The smaller theoretical yield indicates the limiting reactant. 4) Use that amount to calculate the theoretical yield.', 'Follows stoichiometry procedure for limiting reactant in 1.6.', 17),('afa5ada8-656b-4f16-8dda-dd3141cd81a7'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'For the reaction 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O, if 4.00 g of H2 reacts with excess O2, calculate the mass of H2O produced. Provide all steps and final answer with units.', '35.70 g H2O', 'Moles H2 = 4.00 g / 2.016 g/mol = 1.984 mol; Moles H2O = 1.984 mol (1:1 with H2); Mass H2O = 1.984 × 18.015 g/mol = 35.70 g.', 18),('1bed6090-c128-42fe-8c53-217b07b48f17'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'For the reaction 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O, given masses 5.00 g H2 and 8.00 g O2, determine the limiting reagent and the mass of H2O that can be formed. Provide full steps and final units.', 'Limiting reactant: O2; Theoretical yield of H2O: 9.01 g', 'Moles: H2 = 5.00/2.016 = 2.481 mol; O2 = 8.00/32.00 = 0.250 mol. Stoichiometry requires 2 H2 per O2, so O2 is limiting. H2O produced = 0.250 × 2 = 0.500 mol; mass = 0.500 × 18.015 = 9.01 g.', 19),('0a386260-6b48-4fe3-8f66-1e9884c9d398'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which of the following chemical equations is balanced correctly for the formation of aluminum oxide from aluminum and oxygen?', '4Al + 3O2 → 2Al2O3', 'A balanced equation has equal numbers of each type of atom on both sides. The correctly balanced equation is 4Al + 3O2 → 2Al2O3.', 20),('7d41c0f6-8e9d-4362-8ed8-746221acbb8a'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'true_false', 'In a chemical equation, the total number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides.', 'True', 'Conservation of mass requires that atoms are balanced; the same number of each element appears on both sides.', 21),('c1a21c54-24a2-4b22-8849-bc23cb4857e4'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In the reaction Zn + Cu2+ → Zn2+ + Cu, which species is the oxidizing agent?', 'Cu2+', 'The oxidizing agent is the species that is reduced; Cu2+ gains electrons to become Cu.', 22),('1044e866-7202-46c5-8f9b-24e3c1bc9600'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'List the four major types of chemical reactions.', 'Combination, Decomposition, Single displacement, Double displacement', 'The four primary categories of reactions introduced in Unit 1 section 1.3.', 23),('ab30a568-3774-4fa2-8389-42c7a113cd54'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Balance the equation: Al + O2 → Al2O3', '4Al + 3O2 → 2Al2O3', 'Balancing yields 4 Al and 3 O2 on the left and 2 Al2O3 on the right.', 24),('749ca318-de4e-468e-8145-7ccd96d7f67d'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which method is used to balance redox equations by changing oxidation numbers?', 'Oxidation-number-change method', 'Balancing redox equations can be done using the oxidation-number-change method, as described in 1.4.4.', 25),('3b83a354-d3e4-491b-8124-1f0fa24eb623'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In the reaction A + B → AB, if the ratio is 2:1, how many moles of B are required to react with 6 moles of A?', '3 moles', 'A 2:1 ratio (A:B) means 2 moles of A react with 1 mole of B; for 6 moles of A, 3 moles of B are needed.', 26),('6908e8d1-9c47-4495-8912-15f0fd682975'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'Define limiting reactant and explain how to determine it from mole ratios.', 'The limiting reactant is the reactant that is completely consumed first, determining the amount of product formed. It is determined by comparing the available mole ratios to the coefficients in the balanced equation.', 'Limiting reagent concept arises in stoichiometry; identify by mole ratio comparison.', 27),('34d13034-38af-4c02-8821-0715c7fda307'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'The reaction 4 Fe + 3 O2 → 2 Fe2O3 occurs. If 50.0 g Fe and 40.0 g O2 are reacted, what mass of Fe2O3 is produced? (Assume Fe is the limiting reagent.) Also show the steps and units used.', '71.5 g Fe2O3', '1) Moles Fe = 50.0 g / 55.845 g/mol ≈ 0.895 mol. 2) Moles O2 = 40.0 g / 32.00 g/mol = 1.250 mol. 3) Stoichiometry from 4 Fe:3 O2 → 2 Fe2O3. Fe is limiting since required O2 for 0.895 mol Fe is (0.895×3/4)=0.671 mol < 1.250 mol. 4) Moles Fe2O3 formed = 0.895×(2/4)=0.4475 mol. 5) Mass Fe2O3 = 0.4475 mol × 159.69 g/mol ≈ 71.5 g.', 28),('b775ef7b-fd89-4824-825e-1124582f9f40'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'In the reaction 2 H2 + O2 → 2 H2O, if 4.0 g of H2 and 32.0 g of O2 are mixed and allowed to react, what mass of H2O can be formed? Show calculations, units, and the method used.', '35.8 g H2O', 'Molar masses: H2 = 2.016 g/mol, O2 = 31.998 g/mol, H2O = 18.015 g/mol. Moles: n(H2) = 4.0/2.016 ≈ 1.984 mol; n(O2) = 32.0/31.998 ≈ 1.000 mol. Stoichiometry: 2 H2 + 1 O2 → 2 H2O; H2 is limiting since 1.984 mol H2 require 0.992 mol O2, which is ≤ available O2. Moles H2O produced = 1.984 mol. Mass = 1.984 mol × 18.015 g/mol ≈ 35.8 g.', 29),('bec2dd5f-0569-4557-88d3-1cec460420e2'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In a chemical equation, where are the reactants written?', 'Left side', 'Reactants are written on the left side of the equation, with products on the right, separated by an arrow.', 30),('ff50b2a5-990b-49d0-898b-20ecbfe7907a'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'true_false', 'Aqueous solutions are always written as (aq) in chemical equations.', 'True', 'The state symbol (aq) is used to denote an aqueous solution in chemical equations as shown in the section on writing chemical equations.', 31),('1b867480-3c0b-4cb8-8f66-bb904e841bbb'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'State one difference between a chemical reaction and a chemical equation.', 'A chemical reaction is the process that turns reactants into products, while a chemical equation is the symbolic representation of that reaction using symbols and formulas.', 'A reaction describes what happens; the chemical equation shows the reactants and products with balanced relationships.', 32),('46a630ea-f160-4f25-830b-ab2da1defe31'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'If 5.0 g of H2 reacts with 70.0 g of O2 to form water according to the equation 2 H2 + O2 → 2 H2O, what mass of H2O is theoretically produced? Show calculations and give final answer with units.', '44.7 g H2O (approximately)', 'Moles H2 = 5.0 g / 2.016 g/mol ≈ 2.48 mol. From 2 H2 + O2 → 2 H2O, 1 mol H2 yields 1 mol H2O, so total H2O formed ≈ 2.48 mol × 18.02 g/mol ≈ 44.7 g.', 33),('61175a62-6b57-45d3-8418-766dc59c6f2a'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Balance the following equation using the inspection method: Fe + H2O → Fe3O4 + H2. Which balanced equation is correct?', '3Fe + 4H2O → Fe3O4 + 4H2', 'Using inspection balance, Fe atoms, O atoms, and H atoms to obtain 3Fe + 4H2O → Fe3O4 + 4H2.', 34),('1583031b-8764-4fe9-8e29-f97ef6616a35'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In a redox reaction, which statement is true?', 'The oxidizing agent is reduced', 'In redox, the oxidizing agent gains electrons (is reduced) while the reducing agent loses electrons (is oxidized).', 35),('c92a59bf-8516-4fea-8846-9ea73f5edfde'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'For the reaction 2 Al + 3 Cl2 → 2 AlCl3, if you start with 5.0 mol Al and 2.0 mol Cl2, which reactant is limiting and how many moles of AlCl3 can be formed?', 'Limiting reagent: Cl2. Theoretical yield: 1.33 mol AlCl3 (approximately).', 'The mole ratio from the balanced equation shows 3 mol Cl2 produce 2 mol AlCl3. With 2.0 mol Cl2, AlCl3 formed = (2.0)*(2/3) ≈ 1.33 mol. Cl2 is limiting; Al is in excess.', 36),('ae305c62-5604-4a66-8d25-97255595c7b5'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'In the reaction Fe + CuSO4 → FeSO4 + Cu, which species acts as the oxidizing agent?', 'CuSO4', 'Cu2+ in CuSO4 accepts electrons and is reduced; thus CuSO4 is the oxidizing agent.', 37),('fc3a1bec-1640-42ce-82a5-9fe858f391a6'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'fill_blank', 'Explain the difference between synthesis (combination) and decomposition reactions, with one example of each.', 'Synthesis: two or more substances form one product; Decomposition: a compound breaks down into simpler substances (e.g., 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O and 2H2O2 → 2H2O + O2).', 'Synthesis builds up complexity; decomposition breaks down a compound.', 38),('3ce28a79-8117-4e76-88fc-3fadd5c3266c'::uuid, '186f2636-88ce-4dca-8566-df4d36573704'::uuid, 'multiple_choice', 'Which method is used to balance chemical equations by the algebraic method?', 'Algebraic method', 'The algebraic method assigns algebraic coef